## Proof

Suppose \(g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\) satisfies

\[
g(x+g(y))+g(2y)=2x+6y \qquad \text{for all } x,y\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{1}
\]

**Step 1. \(g\) is affine with slope \(2\).**

Fix an arbitrary \(y\in\mathbb{R}\). Rearranging (1),

\[
g(x+g(y)) = 2x + 6y - g(2y) \qquad \text{for all } x\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{2}
\]

As \(x\) ranges over all of \(\mathbb{R}\), the quantity \(t := x + g(y)\) also ranges over all of \(\mathbb{R}\) (since \(g(y)\) is a fixed real number). Substituting \(x = t - g(y)\) into (2) gives

\[
g(t) = 2\bigl(t - g(y)\bigr) + 6y - g(2y)
     = 2t + \bigl(6y - 2g(y) - g(2y)\bigr) \qquad \text{for all } t\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{3}
\]

The left-hand side of (3) does not depend on \(y\), so the bracketed expression \(6y - 2g(y) - g(2y)\) must be a constant, say \(b\in\mathbb{R}\), independent of \(y\). Hence

\[
g(t) = 2t + b \qquad \text{for all } t\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{4}
\]

**Step 2. Substitution yields a contradiction.**

From (4) we compute each term of (1):

\[
g(y) = 2y + b, \qquad g(2y) = 4y + b,
\]
\[
g(x+g(y)) = g(x + 2y + b) = 2(x + 2y + b) + b = 2x + 4y + 3b.
\]

Therefore the left-hand side of (1) equals

\[
g(x+g(y)) + g(2y) = (2x + 4y + 3b) + (4y + b) = 2x + 8y + 4b.
\]

Setting this equal to the right-hand side \(2x + 6y\) forces

\[
8y + 4b = 6y \qquad \text{for all } y\in\mathbb{R},
\]

i.e.,

\[
2y + 4b = 0 \qquad \text{for all } y\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{5}
\]

Taking \(y = 0\) in (5) gives \(b = 0\); substituting back yields \(2y = 0\) for all \(y\), which is false (e.g. \(y = 1\)). This is a contradiction.

**Conclusion.**

The contradiction shows that no function \(g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\) can satisfy equation (1). Equivalently, attempting the natural ansatz \(g(t) = at + b\) gives \(a = 2\) from the \(x\)-coefficient but \(a^{2} + 2a = 8 \neq 6\) from the \(y\)-coefficient, confirming the inconsistency.

\[
\boxed{\text{There is no function } g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R} \text{ satisfying } g(x+g(y))+g(2y)=2x+6y \text{ for all } x,y\in\mathbb{R}.}
\]

